# EVERY CLAIM THAT SPANS A WIDTH, BESIDE WHETHER IT CLAIMS TO HAVE NONE. One row per ranged
# claim anywhere in a document, `violates` where narrowsWhen is `notApplicable`.
#
# ⭐ THE MIRROR OF narrows_a_point_value.sqlc, and the pair is why both are worth having.
#   `notApplicable` on a narrowing means "there is no range here to narrow" -- filed against a
#   claim that demonstrably spans one, it is a category error the document can see for itself.
#
# ⛔⛔ THIS RULE PASSED ON 40 CLAIMS OF 182 WHILE THE CORPUS HELD A VIOLATION IT COULD NOT
#    REACH, and the shape of that violation is exactly what the mirror rule's comment predicts.
#    `every-local-part` filed an elimination of 1800/2000/2400 answering `notApplicable`, under
#    a note pasted verbatim from the elimination beside it -- which is genuinely a point value,
#    2160 exactly, where the same note is true. One document, two claims, and only the width
#    tells them apart. A rule confined to `layer.demand_*` never reaches it; widened to every
#    claim it does, and the honest answer there is `unmeasured`, which is what that filing's
#    own source demand says.
#    ⭐ THE PASSING IDIOM FOR A RANGED ELIMINATION IS IN THE CORPUS TWICE and worth copying:
#      `instrument` with the measurement that would tighten it, over a `none` bound origin --
#      "nothing sets this bound. It is the size of the double counting the composer found".
-- pm:Claim/pm:narrowsWhen with pm:absent/reason = notApplicable on any claim where low <> high.
SELECT r.rule, p.filing, p.layer, p.violates, p.detail
FROM      (
    :compose(checks/roster.sqlc)
) r
LEFT JOIN (
    SELECT c.filing, c.owns || ' claim ' || c.seq AS layer,
           c.narrows_absent IS NOT DISTINCT FROM 'notApplicable' AS violates,
           format('spans %s to %s %s, yet says there is no range to narrow',
                  c.low, c.high, c.unit) AS detail
    FROM (
        :compose(epistemics/ranged_claims.sqlc)
    ) c
) p ON true
WHERE r.slug = 'range_says_no_range'
